WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:05.709
The next service we are going to cover is Network Address Translation, or NAT for short.

00:05.709 --> 00:12.925
NAT allows devices with private IP addresses to use public IP addresses to communicate with other networks.

00:12.925 --> 00:22.128
You may recall from earlier that private IP addresses are not routable, meaning that data from these devices will not route to other networks, namely the Internet.

00:22.128 --> 00:30.563
That is, until NAT appeared on the scene. The most common form of NAT is Port Address Translation, or PAT for short.

00:30.563 --> 00:35.409
In this, any data request from a private IP address is assigned a port number.

00:35.409 --> 00:41.936
The data request is then translated to the public IP address this device has and is routed where it needs to go.

00:41.936 --> 00:45.586
When data is returned, like from the Internet for example,

00:45.586 --> 00:56.040
this device knows the port number for the return data and then translates the address back to the private IP address of the original recipient and sends the data to that address.

00:56.040 --> 01:06.385
With over 65000 port numbers available, this literally allows up to 65000 devices with private IP addresses to share one public IP address for routing purposes.

01:06.385 --> 01:18.025
That is one type of NAT you need to know about for the exam. Here are the other two. Static NAT: a one-to-one mapping between a private IP address and a public IP address.

01:18.025 --> 01:28.025
For this to work, there needs to be a public IP address available for every device with a private IP address. For this reason, it is not commonly used in networks.

01:28.025 --> 01:38.316
Dynamic NAT: a many-to-many mapping between private IP addresses and public IP addresses to where a pool of public IP addresses is used,

01:38.316 --> 01:48.793
and when a device with a private IP address needs to use a public IP address, it does, so long as there is a public IP address available from the pool.

01:48.793 --> 01:55.314
When the connection is done, the public IP address goes back into the pool, waiting to be used again.

01:55.314 --> 02:06.424
PAT is by far the most common form of NAT used, and it is set up by default on many routers, especially small office/home office routers.

